7592186575533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575533 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22134247-39ad-5cd2-bebd-e89dbb4b1f1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575533 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}-(-1))\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}-2)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-1)}{‖∇f(2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2i-j}{\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i-j}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575546 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57b49f13-0a2a-5f52-9cc1-6ee2c2a0903e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575546 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+xy+2x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x+y+2)\\text{ }i+(-2y+x+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1)+2)\\text{ }i+(-2(-1)+1+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,-1)}{‖∇f(1,-1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575562 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "728b8816-964d-5b2c-b1e2-928105ec3a03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575562 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy+8y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2x-2y)\\text{ }i+(2y-2x+8)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =(2(2)-2(-2))\\text{ }i+(2(-2)-2(2)+8)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =8\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-2)}{‖∇f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{8\\,i}{\\sqrt{{8}^{2}}}=i\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575592 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7d1bf90-ec36-5fa3-9648-a21ed4abb91b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575592 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(-1)}{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,-1)}{‖∇f(1,-1)‖} & =\\frac{i-j}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{i-j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575627 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff3d7f48-7543-5dd7-9bd4-9af3c29d885b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575627 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{e}^{xyz}+{y}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(2x+{x}^{2}yz){e}^{xyz}\\,i+({x}^{3}z{e}^{xyz}+2y)\\,j+{x}^{3}y{e}^{xyz}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =(2(1)+({1}^{2})(-2)(0)){e}^{1(-2)(0)}\\,i+(({1}^{3})(0){e}^{1(-2)(0)}+2(-2))\\,j+({1}^{3})(-2){e}^{1(-2)(0)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i-4\\,j-2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
